The critic review for the Josh Hartnett draft is written and passes the pipeline's review schema. I didn't change any other files and didn't publish anything.

**Output:** `docs/content-analysis/pipeline-logs/2026-10-22_06-00-05-196_josh-hartnett/review-critic.json` (8 findings, 5 protected passages). The two hashes are copied from the request and match the frozen draft and evidence when computed the way the pipeline computes them.

**Factual accuracy:** I reopened 13 sources and found no factual errors. Every quote I checked matches its source, and the Batman correction holds up. Two sources wouldn't load (MR PORTER and El País), and I didn't listen to the CBC Q audio, so the two quotes from it rest on the research's machine transcript.

**Main problems:** five findings are major, meaning the type argument is shakier than the prose makes it sound.
1. **Pearl Harbor:** the "he questioned his own fear" story comes from a 2020 interview, 18 years later. The 2002 interviewer wrote that "someone obviously persuaded him," and nothing from 2001–2004 shows him reasoning about fear.
2. **Superman:** the section is titled "The fear that won," but his 2003 answer, which the draft cuts, starts "There's really no specific reason."
3. **Evidence that doesn't separate the types:** meeting the real Malarek was ordinary role research (the Guardian piece says "Hartnett does his homework"). The 2015 clarification and the Weinstein call-back read as normal press damage control. The draft never addresses either reading.
4. **The Type 9 test is applied selectively:** he says "you've just got to let it slide off your back" (2024) and "learn to let it go" (2026), which match the draft's own description of a Nine, and neither appears. The test is also never applied to his biggest choice, stepping back in 2002–03.
5. **"Who gets to define him":** he describes this as something "we all do in our late teens or early twenties" and talks about authenticity, which fits Type 4 or 9 as well as 6. The opening claim that the same few words keep returning has no count behind it, and his longest 2024 explanation uses none of them.

The three minor findings are a trimmed NPR quote that drops "also thrilled that people cared," an over-read of "isn't afraid to go out on a limb," and a Type 7 rebuttal that leans on an unattributed 2024 visa detail.

The draft is honest elsewhere: it keeps his "go along for the ride" quote unexplained, admits that dangerous years could have taught him to be vigilant, and corrects the Batman myth. I marked those passages as worth keeping. Medium confidence is the right ceiling, but the argument still has to earn it.

The similarity check didn't flag anything. The closest matches are shared Type 6 phrasing, not reused arguments.

Risk flags: sensitive allegations (Weinstein, the stalker incident) and contested typing are both on. Historical-source uncertainty and major thesis change are off.
